{"complete":true,"city":"Antwerp","template_id":"global-only-happens-here","title":"Only Happens Here: 10 Things That Make No Sense Anywhere Else","description":"Antwerp turns its own mythology, dialect, printing history, fashion nerve and river engineering into everyday objects: edible hands, dressed saints, basement puppets and a beer named after its glass.","icon":"fingerprint","cards":[{"entity_name":"Antwerpse Handjes","title":"Antwerpse Handjes","subtitle":"The city’s severed-hand legend became a regulated biscuit.","notes":"Created by pastry chef Jos Hakker for a 1934 competition, these almond-topped, hand-shaped biscuits translate the Brabo-and-Antigoon story into something edible. Their recipe, form and packaging were later protected, and production is restricted to members of Antwerp’s bakers’ union—a very Antwerp way to formalize civic folklore.","short_summary":"A protected hand-shaped biscuit born from Antwerp’s founding legend and bakers’ guild.","source_url":"https://mas.be/en/content/antwerp-hand-biscuits-brabo-and-holocaust","source_title":"Antwerp Hand biscuits | Museum MAS","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":null},{"entity_name":"De Poesje van Antwerpen","title":"De Poesje van Antwerpen","subtitle":"Adult puppet theatre survives in a cellar, in broad Antwerp dialect.","notes":"Poesjenellen are rod puppets descended from Italian street theatre and embedded in Antwerp popular culture since the 16th century. The surviving cellar tradition is comic, satirical and emphatically not children’s theatre: characters swear, fight and sing in plat Antwerps. Performances are booked by groups rather than through ordinary individual ticketing.","short_summary":"A basement rod-puppet theatre where satire is still delivered in raw Antwerp dialect.","source_url":"https://mas.be/nl/pagina/de-poesje-van-antwerpen","source_title":"De Poesje van Antwerpen | MAS | Museum aan de Stroom","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"place_id":null,"maps_url":